WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:04.570
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.570 --> 00:00:11.389
Today we are going to take an AI and use it
to synthesize these beautiful, crisp movement

00:00:11.389 --> 00:00:12.669
types.

00:00:12.669 --> 00:00:16.910
And you will see in a moment that this can
do much, much more.

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:19.130
So how does this process work?

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:24.429
There are similar previous techniques that
took a big soup of motion capture data and

00:00:24.429 --> 00:00:28.390
outbid each other what they could learn from
it.

00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:34.810
And they did it really well - for instance,
one these AIs was able to not only learn these

00:00:34.810 --> 00:00:42.660
movements, but even improve them, and even
better, adapt them to different kinds of terrains.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:48.560
This other work used a small training set
of general movements to reinvent popular high

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:53.000
jump technique, the Fosbury flop by itself.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:59.430
This allows the athlete to jump backward over
the bar, thus lowering their center of gravity.

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:02.290
And, it could also do it on Mars.

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:04.160
How cool is that?

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:08.460
But, this new paper takes a different vantage
point.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:13.060
Instead of asking for more and training videos,
it seeks to settle with less.

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:16.010
But first, let’s see what it does.

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:23.230
Yes, we see that this new AI can match reference
movements well, but that’s not all of it.

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:24.370
Not even close.

00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:30.840
The hallmark of a good AI is not being restricted
to just a few movements, but being able to

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:34.500
synthesize to a great variety of different
motions.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:36.740
So, can it do that?

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:43.870
Wow, that is a ton of different kinds of motions,
and the AI always seems to match the reference

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:47.000
motions really well across the board.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:48.000
Bravo!

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:50.970
We’ll talk more about what that means in
a moment.

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:56.840
And here comes the best part, it does not
only generalize to a variety of motions, but

00:01:56.840 --> 00:02:00.420
to a variety of body types as well.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:04.850
And we can bring these body types to different
virtual environments too.

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:07.670
This really seems like the whole package.

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:09.670
And now, if you have been holding on to your
papers, now, squeeze that paper, because we

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:12.499
can control them, in real time!

00:02:12.499 --> 00:02:13.590
My goodness.

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:16.810
So, how does all this this work?

00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:22.510
Let’s see…yes, here, the green is the
target movement we would like to achieve,

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:24.829
and the yellow is the AI’s result.

00:02:24.829 --> 00:02:28.549
Now, here, the trails represent the past.

00:02:28.549 --> 00:02:30.989
So, how close are they?

00:02:30.989 --> 00:02:37.109
Well, of course, we don’t know exactly yet,
so let’s line them up, and…now we’re

00:02:37.109 --> 00:02:38.109
talking!

00:02:38.109 --> 00:02:40.849
They are the almost the same.

00:02:40.849 --> 00:02:43.209
But wait, does this even make sense?

00:02:43.209 --> 00:02:46.519
Aren’t we just inventing a copying machine
here?

00:02:46.519 --> 00:02:51.549
What is so interesting about being able to
copy an already existing movement?

00:02:51.549 --> 00:02:55.249
But, no-no-no, we are not copying here.

00:02:55.249 --> 00:02:56.310
Not even close.

00:02:56.310 --> 00:03:02.359
What this new work does instead is that we
give it the initial pose, and ask it to predict

00:03:02.359 --> 00:03:03.549
the future.

00:03:03.549 --> 00:03:07.790
In particular, we ask what is about to happen
to this model.

00:03:07.790 --> 00:03:10.939
And the result is…a messy trail.

00:03:10.939 --> 00:03:13.280
So, what does this mess mean?

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:19.640
Well, actually, the mess is great news, this
means that the true physics results and the

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:25.549
AI predictions line up so well that they almost
completely cover each other.

00:03:25.549 --> 00:03:30.909
But wait, this is not the first technique
to attempt this…what about previous methods?

00:03:30.909 --> 00:03:32.499
Can they also do this?

00:03:32.499 --> 00:03:35.400
Well, these are all doing pretty good.

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:41.390
Maybe this new work is not that big of an
improvement…wait a second…oh boy.

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:43.659
One contestant is down.

00:03:43.659 --> 00:03:49.489
And now, two have failed, and I love how they
still keep dancing while down.

00:03:49.489 --> 00:03:51.809
A+ for effort, little AIs.

00:03:51.809 --> 00:03:58.490
But the third one, is still in the game…careful…ouch!

00:03:58.490 --> 00:04:01.260
Yup, the new method is absolutely amazing.

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:03.059
No question about it.

00:04:03.059 --> 00:04:08.609
And of course, do not be fooled by these mannequins,
these can be mapped to real characters in

00:04:08.609 --> 00:04:10.549
real video games too.

00:04:10.549 --> 00:04:17.180
So, this amazing new method is able to create
higher quality animations, let us grab a controller

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:21.600
and play with them, and also requires a shorter
training time.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:27.780
Not only that, but the new method predicts
more, and hence, relies much less on the motion

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:32.610
dataset we feed it, and therefore, it is also
less sensitive to its flaws.

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:34.009
I love this.

00:04:34.009 --> 00:05:12.740
A solid step towards democratizing the creation
of superb computer animations.

00:05:12.740 --> 00:05:31.949
What a time to be alive!

00:05:31.949 --> 00:05:35.469
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

